            <h2 id="where">Where to Read or Download It</h2>

            <p>You won't find the 1990 printing in bookstores, but you don't need to hunt for random PDFs either. Three legal routes:</p>

            <ul>
                <li><b>Internet Archive</b>: <a href="https://archive.org/details/schaumsoutlineof0000ayre_o1q8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">archive.org/details/schaumsoutlineof0000ayre_o1q8</a>. A scan of this exact 1990 printing. You can read it online in the browser or borrow the e-copy with a free account, the way you'd borrow a book from a library.</li>
                <li><b>Open Library</b>: <a href="https://openlibrary.org/books/OL2194107M" target="_blank" rel="noopener">openlibrary.org/books/OL2194107M</a>. The catalog page for this edition, with borrow links and reader lists.</li>
                <li><b>WorldCat</b>: <a href="https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/45733539" target="_blank" rel="noopener">search.worldcat.org/oclc/45733539</a>. Enter your location and it shows the nearest libraries holding a physical copy.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Used print copies are another option: the 3rd edition sold in big numbers and turns up cheap on the second-hand market.</p>

            <h2 id="organized">How the Book Is Organized</h2>

            <p>Every chapter follows the same three beats: a short theory block, a <b>Solved Problems</b> section, and a <b>Supplementary Problems</b> section. The solved problems carry the book — that's where you watch each technique applied — and since answers to all problems are printed in the book, the supplementary set works as a self-test. Because the theory is kept brief, the chapters line up with almost any calculus course, whatever textbook it uses.</p>

